1 WRITE

   Writes one record to a file: the values of its expressions, one after
   another.

   Format

     WRITE logical-name[:] expression[,...]

2 Parameters

 logical-name[:]

   A file OPEN opened to write, or SYS$OUTPUT, or SYS$ERROR or
   SYS$COMMAND, which are the terminal even when a procedure's output
   goes to an /OUTPUT file.

 expression[,...]

   The record's contents (see Symbol_Assign Expressions).  An integer
   is written in decimal.  The qualifiers come before the data.

2 Description

   On an indexed file, WRITE puts the record where its keys say, in any
   order (RMS-F-DUP for a key that allows no duplicates), but after
   OPEN/APPEND the first record must come after the file's last
   (RMS-F-SEQ).

   A record WRITE can't write is RMS's error (RMS-F-NEF for a
   /READ/WRITE file READ hasn't read to its end).  WRITE leaves $STATUS
   RMS$_NORMAL, or on an indexed file RMS$_OK_DUP for a record whose
   alternate key another record has already.

2 Qualifiers
/ERROR=label

   In a command procedure, goes to the label if the record can't be
   written.

/SYMBOL

   Lets an item's value be longer than 255 characters.  Without
   /SYMBOL, a longer one is DCL-W-TKNOVF.

/UPDATE

   Replaces the record READ read last, with one of the same length.  On
   an indexed file the record may be another length, but may not change
   the primary key (RMS-F-CHG).

2 Examples

   1.$ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Today is ", F$TIME()

     Writes the date and time to the terminal.

   2.$ WRITE/UPDATE PARTS "WIDGET    ", COUNT

     Replaces the record of PARTS that READ read last.
